{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451463-entropy-affecting-others-good-bad-luck-mechanics","id":1451463,"title":"Entropy: affecting others / good & bad luck mechanics","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-09T11:48:37-07:00","id":1451463,"title":"Entropy: affecting others / good & bad luck mechanics","content":"Hello. <br /> <br /> I have had a hard time finding how to use Entropy-influenced &quot;good luck&quot; and &quot;bad luck&quot;, system-wise. Specially problematic when one of your players uses it all the time...<br /> <br /> So, my question(s):<br /> <br /> If you bless or curse someone with good or bad luck... do you rise or reduce the difficulty of some actions depending on the successes you get on an Arete roll? Do you use successes or failures (I guess &quot;ones&quot;)?<br /> <br /> What if a character blesses himself with good luck for a whole day? Would the reduction or successes apply to any roll he does that day?<br /> <br /> HDYDT doesnt have any rule regarding that. <br /> <br /> *Additional situation and question:<br /> There's a guy throwing knives as part of a circus act. The character who uses Entropy (an spectator) wants to affect the result of that act, I guess blessing the knife-thrower or the knives themselves, which would be manipulating someone else's luck or an objects luck...is that Entropy 2 or 3?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5394-argonot","name":"Argonot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5394_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T13:10:27-07:00","id":1451478,"title":"","content":"Generally, you modify difficulty, on yourself or on someone else.<br /> <br /> There have been success modifying example effects in previous editions, but M20 seems to have dropped those in favor of using successes on your Arete roll to influence outcomes to your desires with more successes needed based on how complex and impactful what you're trying to do is.<br /> <br /> So your choices to &quot;bless&quot; a knife-throwing act (at this level, person and object can both be impacted) are to reduced the difficulty of the act up to -3, so it's much easier, but not a guaranteed success, or to directly nudge the act with the ST determining how many successes you need to ensure that the knives all land without harming the assistant. I would interpret that as Entropy 2 and Entropy 3 respectively, but you could make the case that both fall under Entropy 2 (depends on how you want to interpret the randomness of events as a factor).<br /> <br /> As for duration? Entropy is written that you impact a specific action (at least until much high levels and then it gets more narrative and less mechanical anyway). So you could make yourself lucky at knowing just the right thing to say for the day for a reduction on social difficulties that exploit emotional weaknesses to get people to agree with you, but it would be a separate effect to make yourself lucky at punching people for the day. At Entropy 4 things get looser though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-09T15:02:31-07:00","id":1451498,"title":"","content":"When I ran Mage, Revised or M20, I ruled that bonuses/penalties to a roll converted from Arete successes on a 1:1 basis. I allowed for adding phantom successes or botches converted on a 2:1 basis, meaning that each success or botch die added to a roll required two successes. The latter effect being significantly more powerful, as it ruled out the chance of critical failure or added for all intents and purposes a success threshold regardless of target number.<br /> <br /> If I remember correctly, I required Entropy 1 for self-buffing, Entropy 2 to target others with bonuses or penalties, and Entropy 3 to target others with phantom successes/failures.<br /> <br /> As far as duration, targets, and broadening the effect, duration and targeting is already in the book. As far as broadening the effect, I quantified it by ability, each ability required an additional success each.<br /> <br /> So, the way I ruled it, for a character to give themselves a phantom success on Melee rolls for a scene, they would need four successes. If they wanted to broaden that to Athletics rolls, five. If they wanted to do that for another character, targeting as per normal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-09-2021, 06:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-11T06:38:43-07:00","id":1451651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1451463-entropy-affecting-others-good-bad-luck-mechanics?p=1451498#post1451498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I ran Mage, Revised or M20, I ruled that bonuses/penalties to a roll converted from Arete successes on a 1:1 basis. I allowed for adding phantom successes or botches converted on a 2:1 basis, meaning that each success or botch die added to a roll required two successes. The latter effect being significantly more powerful, as it ruled out the chance of critical failure or added for all intents and purposes a success threshold regardless of target number.<br /> <br /> If I remember correctly, I required Entropy 1 for self-buffing, Entropy 2 to target others with bonuses or penalties, and Entropy 3 to target others with phantom successes/failures.<br /> <br /> As far as duration, targets, and broadening the effect, duration and targeting is already in the book. As far as broadening the effect, I quantified it by ability, each ability required an additional success each.<br /> <br /> So, the way I ruled it, for a character to give themselves a phantom success on Melee rolls for a scene, they would need four successes. If they wanted to broaden that to Athletics rolls, five. If they wanted to do that for another character, targeting as per normal.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Thank you very much. This sounds like a good way to run it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5394-argonot","name":"Argonot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5394_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"227"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}